From: Darrel Griet <dgriet@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Jul 2026 10:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] v10: Deliver update requests immediately on minnow

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [minnow command-mode panel specific]

The shared v10 backend (0009) delivers scene-graph update requests from
the hwcomposer vsync callback, phase-locking render to the display. On
minnow that adds up to a full vsync period of latency between an input
event dirtying the scene and the render being delivered: measured
average requestUpdate->deliver latency was ~15 ms (max ~one period).

Now that swap() no longer waits for vsync before set() (the panel
self-paces through its TE signal), that phase-locking buys nothing on
throughput - frame cadence stays panel-limited at ~45 fps - while the
delivery delay is pure input latency. Delivering on a zero-interval
timer (next event-loop pass) instead drops the measured delivery
latency to ~0.7 ms with no cadence regression (two-vsync-period frames
during animation stayed at 0%). This is the difference that makes
touch dragging feel responsive.

Only correct in combination with the minnow swap() TE self-pacing, so
it is a minnow-only change layered on the shared backend.

---
--- a/hwcomposer/hwcomposer_backend_v10.cpp
+++ b/hwcomposer/hwcomposer_backend_v10.cpp
@@ -376,16 +376,19 @@
     if (m_displayOff && !m_ambientMode)
         return true;
 
-    // Deliver update requests from the vsync callback (see notifyVsync), so
-    // rendering is phase-locked to the display instead of paced by a timer
-    // that drifts against it. The scene graph only asks for another update if
-    // something actually changed, so a static scene still stops rendering.
-    m_updatePending.storeRelease(1);
-
-    // Fallback in case vsync events do not arrive (e.g. around display
-    // off/on transitions); mirrors the v11/v20 vsync timeout strategy.
+    // Deliver the update on the next event-loop pass rather than holding it
+    // back to the vsync callback (which notifyVsync would do on the general
+    // v10 path). hwc_device->set() already self-paces against the panel's TE
+    // signal now that swap() no longer waits for vsync, so throughput stays
+    // panel-limited (~45 fps) either way - but delivering immediately removes
+    // up to one vsync period of input-to-render latency (measured ~15 ms avg
+    // -> ~0.7 ms), which is what makes dragging feel responsive. The scene
+    // graph only requests another update when something actually changed, so a
+    // static scene still stops rendering and the SoC can idle. The
+    // m_updatePending / notifyVsync vsync-delivery path is thus unused on
+    // minnow; it is left in place for the shared v10 backend.
     if (!m_deliverUpdateTimeout.isActive())
-        m_deliverUpdateTimeout.start(50, this);
+        m_deliverUpdateTimeout.start(0, this);
 
     return true;
 }
